I just think that it's unacceptable to  have the the folks who control the pipes  get into anything that smacks of  controlling the content or favoring  their content over other god whoa if  you're like me you've spent the last few  months pondering one of life's elusive  questions what the hell is net  neutrality I posed this question to Ruiz  an associate editor and resident net  neutrality guru Peter Sirmon who  explained it this way Internet service  providers like AT&amp;T or Comcast should be  required to treat all pieces of  information that pass through their  pipes or over their airwaves equally you  know that sounds pretty good I mean who  could object to everyone being treated  equally on the Internet  it turns out a whole bunch of people  agree like Mallory from family ties what  if there was a show that Time Warner had  coming out that Warner had coming out  they were gonna show online it was very  similar to show I had online and our  show is very successful and maybe and  this is just a supposition maybe they  didn't want that  maybe they turn to me and say unless you  pay us $10,000 a month we're gonna make  the cut of the connection to your  website very slow and then there's that  vegetarian guy who's always getting  beaten up by Eminem fans don't people  know if their ability to download  information from the internet will be  severely compromised if net neutrality  is overturned but when I found out that  Al Gore was a supporter of net  neutrality I started getting a little  suspicious whoa  so according to Malory and Moby the  Internet could be threatened by ISPs  like Comcast and Verizon who might slow  down traffic and may perhaps cut off  access to content they disagree with  ensure it sounds like a terrifying  dystopian future but here's the problem  none of this is actually happening net  neutrality proponents must rely on  hypotheticals because there simply  aren't many examples of supposed  neutrality violations  what a few cases cited as Pearl Jam  frontman Eddie Vedder's anti-bush lyrics  during an AT&amp;T sponsored web stream the  company cut out the political statements  sudirman explains it shouldn't happened  and everybody knows it shouldn't happen  it was it was a myth you know it was a  human error it wasn't policy but other  than that there very very few examples  let's say that net neutrality activists  succeed in regulating the Internet just  who would do the regulating it seems  that job would fall to the FCC one thing  that's particularly problematic with the  FCC's stance on that neutrality is that  what they're proposing in addition to  the guidelines is a call it as they see  it standard so that there's no  particular set of rules instead the art  the FCC will get to be the arbiter of  what is okay and what isn't on a  case-by-case basis so they want the FCC  the Janet Jackson Nipplegate people the  people who find bono for saying to  regulate the internet rather than  getting government bureaucracy involved  in a problem that doesn't yet exist  sudirman suggests we stick to the market  which has worked exceptionally well so  far is peas aren't perfect but at least  they're subject to market pressures the  government on the other hand is subject  primarily to political pressures most of  the impetus for net neutrality is a  result of political pressure big  influential actors pushing the  government to do what they want to  protect their business models look it's  because the internet isn't shackled by  government regulations that is expanded  it's such a rapid rate and it's because  of the profit motive that access to  high-speed broadband has exploded in  recent years increased regulation when  no one can even point to real-world  examples of ISPs infringing on network  neutrality runs the very real risk of  stifling innovation in slowing future  growth so let's take a page from Al Gore  whoa and slow down and wait until  there's a real problem before we start  discussing regulation for reason TV I'm  Michael Moynihan
 

